        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">It is almost always static</strong>: charge built up by belt
            friction and rubber soles, released through your fingertip at the console. Dry air,
            carpet and a poorly grounded outlet are the usual culprits, and a humidifier, a mat
            and a properly grounded outlet fix most cases.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Never defeat the ground pin</strong>, never spray the belt or
            near the motor, and treat any tingle, buzz or shock that does not behave like static as
            a fault — unplug it and have it checked. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">troubleshooting guide</a>
